    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      <em>Bedroom Dialectics</em> is mostly just me trying to figure out what I actually think about things before going to sleep.
    </p>

    <p>
      My name is <strong>Muhammad Faza</strong>. I keep this site as a quiet place to write down observations that feel too slow or awkward for the internet's main channels. When you keep thoughts trapped in your head, they tend to bounce around and sound much smarter or heavier than they actually are. Putting them into plain sentences—word by word, late at night—usually knocks the ego out of them and reveals whatever modest truth was hiding underneath.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is no editorial board, no grand publication strategy, and no fixed genre. Some pieces are in English, some in Bahasa Indonesia. One day it might be a reflection on why running shoes got so needlessly complicated, the next it might be an inquiry into colonial train tickets, physical infrastructure, or just the quiet rhythm of sitting in a room after everyone else has gone to sleep.
    </p>

    <p>
      None of this is meant to be definitive advice or intellectual posturing. It is just an ongoing record of paying attention to the ordinary friction of being alive, and working through things with whatever clarity is available at the moment.
    </p>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>

    <p>
      This site is built with <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Astro</a>, rendered in pure static HTML and CSS, and set in <em>IBM Plex Mono</em>. No tracking scripts, no popups, no cookies, and no algorithm trying to maximize your time on page.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you want to follow along quietly, there is an <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a>. If something here resonated with you—or if you think I got something completely wrong—feel free to reach out.
    </p>
  </div>
